#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078279 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/id #uuid "06d5e05a-4595-551f-8c1b-7c585f69b47b" :question/math-academy-id "e-74" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x - 2\\sqrt{x}} {x - 4}}$." :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to 4^{+}}\\frac{x - 2\\sqrt{x}}{x - 4} &= \\frac{4 - 2\\sqrt{4}}{4 - 4} = \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x - 2\\sqrt{x}} {x - 4} &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {(x -2 \\sqrt{x})(x+2\\sqrt x)} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x^2-\\left(2\\sqrt{x}\\right)^2} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x^2-4x} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x(x-4)} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x} {x+2\\sqrt x} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {4} {4+2\\sqrt4} \\\\ &=\\dfrac 1 2. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078280 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "7db311cd-70a0-50f4-8984-4a578abc67eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-75" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac {3x} {\\sqrt{x +1} -1}}$." :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to 0}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{x + 1} - 1} &= \\frac{3 \\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{0 + 1} - 1} = \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac {3x} {\\sqrt{x + 1} - 1} &=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(3x)(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{(\\sqrt{x + 1} - 1)(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)} \\\\ &=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{\\left(\\sqrt{x+1}\\right)^2-1^2} \\\\ &=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{x+1-1} \\\\ &=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{x} \\\\ &=\\lim_{x\\to 0}3(\\sqrt{x+1}+1) \\\\ &=3(\\sqrt{0+1}+1) \\\\ &=6. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5b4e8a5-a2cc-5f7c-9777-ff4b3cf574bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264522} {:answer/id #uuid "a5cfa902-4e4d-535a-9d48-b74e92e2614f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5b4e8a5-a2cc-5f7c-9777-ff4b3cf574bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264522} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc7bd336-dc82-597d-938b-ca1bda73e817" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f682dc1-21d5-50c6-a843-a82f4d54fb3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338515" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{z-6}{\\sqrt{3z-2}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $z=6,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{z-6}{\\sqrt{3z-2}-4} & =\\frac{6-6}{\\sqrt{3(6)-2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{z-6}{\\sqrt{3z-2}-4} & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{(\\sqrt{3z-2}-4)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{(\\sqrt{3z-2})^{2}-{4}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{(3z-2)-16} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{3z-18} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{3(z-6)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{\\sqrt{3z-2}+4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(6)-2}+4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a33edddc-1da0-5d41-a93b-892dcdc07dc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076258} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8cefe5-a5a0-5541-932b-e9e5fbe534ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076259} {:answer/id #uuid "df7ddb01-dc80-5476-aa94-7fc2b5a0142e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076260} {:answer/id #uuid "2d727eff-da34-5178-8fa7-980bad27e56b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076261} {:answer/id #uuid "63eb7fd8-208e-52bd-8b1c-8c3561420d5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd8cefe5-a5a0-5541-932b-e9e5fbe534ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076259} :answer-field/id #uuid "a542e28e-89c7-5fa7-9efa-480f95f1bcb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076257}] :question/id #uuid "999c69b4-b8e1-5935-8b29-a7c23a4a8300" :question/math-academy-id "q-4463" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x + 4} - 2}{x}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8298da89-4e8a-5c30-9d6a-3fa48a89ff90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076264} {:answer/id #uuid "bccaf12b-6c9b-585b-84f9-0268e20d16f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076265} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9ad3e0-dcfd-5a40-89f8-13bb63dad038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076266} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0b29b4-ffb0-543e-9824-22aad58d4ba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076267} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea9b4f1-cf27-556d-b1b6-b28ad28fedf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bccaf12b-6c9b-585b-84f9-0268e20d16f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076265} :answer-field/id #uuid "390ea65e-f475-5aa4-85d6-c92c4650e8e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fb5c930-d58c-5aeb-b5e0-b4a28b9ae989" :question/math-academy-id "q-4464" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 1^{-}}\\frac{x - 1}{\\sqrt{x + 3} - 2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8177625e-0fcd-5d78-8f77-7ad6c29a7ae2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264533} {:answer/id #uuid "e658e508-987f-5a4f-8f2e-ec2f8203d053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264534} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2e0f3a-8505-59a5-925d-db93ef752c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264535} {:answer/id #uuid "22179b09-8761-585b-871e-5bb6d504d869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264536} {:answer/id #uuid "3e47abe8-ec8c-5c9c-b680-47a9eb83e40c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e47abe8-ec8c-5c9c-b680-47a9eb83e40c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264537} :answer-field/id #uuid "846cf5ca-7696-5b32-a145-e9f822d54432" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51433638-23de-5865-9a91-de5431948eea" :question/math-academy-id "q-46831" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2+2}-2}{{2}^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+2}-2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x+2})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x+2-4}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{1}{(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2+2)(\\sqrt{2+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
